Here is another interesting book on the idea that each individual 
consciousness is really a melange of separate consciousnesses.  Note the 
capsule review from A-albionic Research in which Lloyd Miller was 
relating this phenomenon to his theory of social organisms which is 
related to the original post to this list on loss in individuality and 
miscability, etc.


Social Brain Discovering the Networks of the Mind by Michael S. Gazzaniga 
 year 1985 pp 219 hc/pb Hardcover Pric $15.00

Many object to the concept of "social organism" on the basis of opposing
elements within an organism...for instance left-wing versus right-wing
Catholics or liberal versus conservative Jews.  However, this argument
betrays an ignorance of how fragmented the individual's consciousness is! 

Gazzaniga demonstates how each individual mind contains a whole array of
distinct modules or personalities.  In the "metally ill" this can display
itself as actual "multiple personalities".  In the healthy mind, the
various modules slide smoothly in and out of control of the organism
according to need with no sharp breaks in consciousness.  In other words,
the Vatican has its uses for its left and right forces just as the
individual has uses for its peaceful and violent modules. 

