From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Sun, 18 Sep 1994 02:45:55 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-232] what's next?

Adam:
> I do, however, think that with existing technologies and fair
> degree of organizational power, some fantastic ideas (that already exist,
> and as nick says, are "rigorously theoretical") can be made real.

"Organizational power" is a slippery thing.  The businesssmen and
politicians you excoriate make organizational power their life's 
work.  We could learn quite a lot from them, rather than
futiley complaining about them.  It's a grave handicap to the
Extropian movement that more people experienced in business
management are not involved.  We applaud free enterprise from 
the bleachers; they play the game.  We do have a few technological
entrepreneurs, of course; but if we have any MBAs or CEOs of major 
companies, or anybody with major experience running the finances
of a large business, they have not spoken up.

Coincidentally, I'm reading a great book that sheds quite a bit
of light on individuals vs. organizations, cooperation vs. competition,
and similar topics that come up here frequently: _Games, Strategies,
and Manangers_ by John McMillan.  It's an introduction to game
theory with a lot of business practicum wrapped in.  Highly 
recommended.

Theory, no matter how rigorous, is often a _long_ ways from
being practical in the near future.  I've started developing what 
might be called "laws of memetics" which gauge the probabilities and 
rates of cultural change, including technological innovation.  One basic 
hypothesis that's so far been verified is this: technological 
change, like any other kind of evolution, is _at the level of 
information_ a very gradual process.  There has never been rapid 
informational change; only fluxes of randomness combined with
rare instances of gradual evolutionary progress.  Memetics has
sped up evolution well beyond genetics, but it is still quite
slow compared to some Extropians' expectations.  

1,000*, 1 million*, etc. faster computers will speed up some kinds of change 
while others remain slow.  A fundamental area of study here is what kinds 
of structures can be adapted by evolution and other search processes
in polynomial time, and which are superpolynomial: with average
case, worst case, and "probably approximately correct (PAC)" case 
analysis.  The worst and cases are the best studied; many kinds of basic 
computing structures (such as finite automata, and many important algebraic
structures) require superpolynomial time, alas.  Quite often learning,
adapation and innovation prove as difficult as cryptanalysis.  Average 
case analysis may in some cases prove kinder.   

Of course, locally we can often see rapid change, due to (1) rapid 
conversion from one existing memeset to another existing memeset, or (2) 
fortuitous but basically simple recombinations of existing memesets.  
The creation of new memesets (including new technological innovations) 
is very slow in comparison to these local punctuations.

With respect to theoretical applied science, it is relatively
easy to, for example, work out the basic physics of
a piece of nanotechnology (although no one has found _Nanosystems_
a cakewalk).  Actually creating the wide array of tools needed to build 
such machines, starting with today's tools, is a vastly more 
sophisticated task than simply designing the machines.  As
we get faster computers and better software, it will become still
easier to make such theoretical designs.  The gap between
theory and practice will grow; and the results of theoretical
applied science will increasingly dwarf practice in its
(theorized) capabilities.  The good news is that doesn't slow 
down technological progress; rather it gives us a much better 
picture of the possibilities in our far future.  Drexler's
work is the first major step in that direction.

When businessmen and engineers in real technology fields such as 
medicine, biotech, and semiconductors see amateurs cavalierly 
dismissing them as today's sources of technological innovation, 
in favor of theoretical vaporware that is nowhere near practicality, 
it's no wonder that they are turned off by futurist movements
like Extropianism.  It's extremely unfortunate, because capabilities 
of life extension, especially cryonics, now make far future technology
important to us all, regardless of how impractical these
technologies are today.  We should be clear in drawing the
distinction between _theoretical technology important for 
deciding our fate_ and _technology of practical use today and 
in the near future_.  They're quite different: very different
kinds of critical analysis and conclusions are called for.
Unfortunately, eager believers with no sense of history
tend to mix these quite distinct studies together.  In a bout of 
millenial fever they project space colonization, assembler singularities, 
and other such radical advances in  our near future.  In the process, 
they turn off most practical technological and memetic talent
to futurism in general, and Extropianism in particular.  By
mixing up theoretical studies of the far future with practical
engineering of today's technology, we invite critique of the
former by the hard-nosed, practical, net present value, 
and sophisticated standards of the the latter.  Not surprisingly,
theoretical technology, with little economic analysis and
no tooling path from today's technology, comes up severely
wanting against today's technology, and small productive changes 
to present technology.  But it comes up wanting by the wrong
standards.  Net present value should become net 2100 value.
Lack of completely specified tooling path from 1994 to 2100 is far 
less important than lack of complete tooling path from 1994 to 2004,
which can stop a project cold.  Yet we confound the two kinds
of technological analyses, and shoot ourselves in the foot in
the process.

Instead of gee-whizzing each other about how banal today's technology
is compared with our theories and dreams, we should be communcating 
the fact that Extropianism is the first worldview that gives
gives a personal reason to care about the far future, a reason that has 
little connection to the reasons people have heretofore cared about 
the future: personal immortality.  Before Extropianism 
futurism was a study that cared not about the future of people,
forsaking the future of individuals for utopian visions of Society.
It was rightfully disdained by all wise businessfolk.  Today
businessfolk and all other people have reason to pay attention: not because 
their businesses are working on the wrong technologies (it's just as 
idiotic for us to make that claim as it was for futurists in times 
past), but because they can have something personally at stake in 
the far future.

